1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,996
Did you know that molecules like to make friends
just like you do?

2
00:00:03,996 --> 00:00:04,934
They come together

3
00:00:04,934 --> 00:00:05,724
change partners

4
00:00:05,724 --> 00:00:06,762
and even take breaks!

5
00:00:06,762 --> 00:00:10,451
Let me tell you the story of how they do all this

6
00:00:10,451 --> 00:00:10,753
First

7
00:00:10,753 --> 00:00:13,525
imagine two little molecules meeting for the first
time

8
00:00:13,525 --> 00:00:15,525
This is called synthesis

9
00:00:15,525 --> 00:00:18,768
They get together and decide to become one
happy group

10
00:00:18,768 --> 00:00:19,829
just like when two

11
00:00:19,829 --> 00:00:23,210
pieces of a puzzle fit perfectly to make a whole
picture

12
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:24,160
This happens when

13
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:24,844
for instance

14
00:00:24,844 --> 00:00:26,899
hydrogen and oxygen meet to make water!

15
00:00:26,899 --> 00:00:27,109
Now

16
00:00:27,109 --> 00:00:29,051
sometimes friends need a little space

17
00:00:29,051 --> 00:00:30,864
In a process called decomposition

18
00:00:30,864 --> 00:00:32,835
a molecule decides to split into two

19
00:00:32,835 --> 00:00:33,048
just

20
00:00:33,048 --> 00:00:37,045
like when you break a cookie into halves to share
with a buddy

21
00:00:37,045 --> 00:00:40,734
This happens when water decides to separate
back into hydrogen and oxygen

22
00:00:40,734 --> 00:00:43,192
Sometimes molecules like to have a dance party!

23
00:00:43,192 --> 00:00:44,454
In single replacement

24
00:00:44,454 --> 00:00:47,496
one friend steps in to dance with one of the
partners

25
00:00:47,496 --> 00:00:48,326
switching places

26
00:00:48,326 --> 00:00:49,496
just like musical chairs

27
00:00:49,496 --> 00:00:53,115
It's like zinc deciding to step in and dance with
some sulfur

28
00:00:53,115 --> 00:00:54,107
leaving copper on

29
00:00:54,107 --> 00:00:56,107
its own

30
00:00:56,107 --> 00:00:57,490
Then there’s double replacement

31
00:00:57,490 --> 00:00:58,875
where everyone finds new friends

32
00:00:58,875 --> 00:01:01,027
Imagine two pairs of friends swapping partners

33
00:01:01,027 --> 00:01:05,638
It’s like a friendship swap where everyone finds
a new buddy to hang out with

34
00:01:05,638 --> 00:01:09,019
just like silver and chlorine trading partners with
sodium and nitrate

35
00:01:09,019 --> 00:01:11,325
There are other exciting things molecules do

36
00:01:11,325 --> 00:01:11,478
too

37
00:01:11,478 --> 00:01:12,737
Like during combustion

38
00:01:12,737 --> 00:01:15,037
where a fuel burns in the presence of air

39
00:01:15,037 --> 00:01:15,474
creating

40
00:01:15,474 --> 00:01:15,887
energy

41
00:01:15,887 --> 00:01:18,242
like the warmth you feel from a campfire

42
00:01:18,242 --> 00:01:18,727
Sometimes

43
00:01:18,727 --> 00:01:21,493
molecules decide to settle down and form
something solid

44
00:01:21,493 --> 00:01:21,930
like when

45
00:01:21,930 --> 00:01:24,082
raindrops turn into hailstones – that's
precipitation!

46
00:01:24,082 --> 00:01:26,849
And let's not forget when acids and bases meet

47
00:01:26,849 --> 00:01:28,322
They balance each other out

48
00:01:28,322 --> 00:01:29,531
forming salt and water

49
00:01:29,531 --> 00:01:30,846
almost like mixing paints

50
00:01:30,846 --> 00:01:32,846
to create a new color

51
00:01:32,846 --> 00:01:36,063
Next time you’re playing with friends or
building with blocks

52
00:01:36,063 --> 00:01:36,841
think about how

